window.COACH = {"page": "laws-and-regulations", "title": "Laws and Regulations", "prizeName": "Integrated Human Practices, Safety and Security", "summary": {"verdict": "This is already a stronger regulatory page than anything in the casebook on structure: the system is split into three components, every classification is hedged, and a whole section lists what is still unconfirmed. It falls behind the winners on people and on proof. One industry briefing stands in for all stakeholders, no regulator was asked anything, no rule is cited by article, and no design change on the page is traced to a rule. Several claims also disagree with the Entrepreneurship and Geospatial Analysis pages, which is the fastest way to lose a judge's trust on legal writing.", "strengths": ["The three-component split (bioreactor, culture, cell-free output), held consistently from section 1 to section 9.", "Honest hedging: \"a potential pathway for ReLeaf's cell-free output, but not an automatic classification\", and a review that says what it could not find.", "Section 10, which lists every unconfirmed item in one table instead of hiding them.", "Kelpak and Sagarika as real products that show classification changing by place and by time.", "The sentence that ties engineering to law: containment determines the exposure pathway regulators must consider.", "Figure 3's caption, which names the source, her title, the date and how the information was used.", "40 C.F.R. Part 725 cited where the claim is made, and the Taiwanese category given in Chinese and English."], "priorities": ["Fix the page-to-page contradictions first, starting with the market order, which the new abstract sheet now shows on the first screen: EU second here against the Philippines first and the EU at year 5.5 on Entrepreneurship. Then the 30-day fertilizer timeline and \"absolute containment\" certification on Entrepreneurship, ACCD heat against the salinity evidence and the organic exclusion on Geospatial Analysis, the missing NaD1, and BoPEP4 against BoPep4.", "Turn section 8 into a real stakeholder entry, and add at least one regulator and one critic. Ask the two questions WageningenUR asked: does each molecule need its own approval, and what residual cell or DNA limit applies to the output.", "Name one design change that a rule caused, and write it where a judge will see it. Your Human Practices page already records the move away from direct soil release; that is a legal decision and it belongs here with a link.", "Cite rules by article with a link and an access date, and give every unconfirmed item an owner and a next step.", "Cut the repetition: the opening now appears three times (abstract sheet, section 1, start of section 2), the molecule list six times, the Kelpak table is retold in prose, and \"rather than\" carries 18 sentences. Let the sheet carry the summary and cut section 1 to the lines the sheet does not have.", "Summarise this page in one line with a link on Human Practices, Safety and Sustainability. Judges only reach it from pages with a Standard URL, and today only Entrepreneurship names it."], "ballot": [{"question": "How well was the Human Practices work integrated throughout the project?", "status": "partial", "where": "2. Regulation as product design; 4. Function to claim to classification", "note": "The page argues that regulation shaped the build, and Table 1 states it as a principle, but no single design decision on the page is traced to a named rule."}, {"question": "How inspiring an example is it to others?", "status": "partial", "where": "3. Three components, three regulatory questions; 10. Exploring the ambiguity", "note": "The component split and the unconfirmed table are reusable by any team with a contained production system; the research method behind them is never described."}, {"question": "To what extent is the Human Practices work documented so that others can build upon it?", "status": "partial", "where": "6. Expansion markets; References", "note": "Eleven markets with named instruments is real documentation, but no claim carries an article number, links are bare domains, and nothing has an access date."}, {"question": "How thoughtfully was it implemented? How well did they explain the context, rationale and prior work?", "status": "strong", "where": "4.1 EU reference point; 7. What existing products teach us; 9. Where does ReLeaf sit?", "note": "Prior work arrives as two comparable products, and the rationale chain from function to market access is stated and then followed."}, {"question": "How well did it incorporate different stakeholder views?", "status": "missing", "where": "8. Industrial perspective: Cheng Han Biotechnology", "note": "One industry briefing, with the name only in a figure caption. No regulator, no lawyer, no organic farmer and no critic of GM agriculture appears."}], "skeleton": {"releaf": ["1. Abstract", "2. Regulation as product design", "3. Three components, three regulatory questions", "4. Function to claim to classification (4.1 EU reference point)", "5. Three target markets (Taiwan, EU, US)", "6. Expansion markets", "7. What existing products teach us (Kelpak, Sagarika)", "8. Industrial perspective: Cheng Han Biotechnology", "9. Where does ReLeaf sit?", "10. Exploring the ambiguity (what is still unconfirmed?)", "11. Our regulatory strategy", "References"], "winners": ["Open with the questions the page answers, or with the rule that is about to change the design", "One entry per rule, five slots each: the rule, what it requires, what it means for a named component, who confirmed it, what the team changed", "A comparable product already on the market as the anchor for the argument", "Unresolved classifications written as conditionals, with a date and a status", "The design change repeated on the lab, hardware or business page it changed", "A one-line summary and a link on every page that has a Standard URL"], "gap": "Your skeleton is built by jurisdiction and component, which the winners never manage, and it reads well. What it has no room for is the entry: a named person, the question you asked, the answer, and the change that followed. Sections 8 and 10 are where those entries belong, and both currently stop before the person and the change."}, "extras": ["\"Regulation is therefore not the final checkpoint of ReLeaf - it is one of the constraints that shape what we build\" is the third time section 2 says this. Cut the sentence.", "The three bullets in section 8 (\"Product classification should be considered early.\" and the two after it) repeat Table 1's three principles. Replace them with what Yuan Chiu-Ying actually told you.", "\"Our goal is not simply to identify a legal category after the product is built\" repeats sections 1 and 2 again. Keep one closing sentence.", "The table in section 4 heads its first column \"Biostimulant\" although the paragraph above says no authority has classified the molecules. Rename the column \"Molecule\".", "Headings run together: \"1.Abstract\", \"4.1EU reference point\". Put a space after each number.", "\"Cheng Han Biotechnology's Break Down of Different Global Market's Legal Authorities.\" Breakdown is one word, and it should be \"Markets' Legal Authorities\".", "\"Table 1. Three Principles Shaped Our Development\" reads as a half sentence. Write \"Table 1. Three principles that shaped our development.\"", "\"#### Cell-free output\" is used as a heading under both Taiwan and the United States. Add the market to each one.", "\"'Title 40 of the Code of Federal Regulations Part 725'\" does not need the quotation marks. Drop them, and cite Section 725.424 where you mention the exemption, since it is already in your references.", "In the Malaysia row, \"Fertilizer inputs are subject to agricultural and product-control requirements, but the exact route for ReLeaf's formulation requires further investigation\" sits in the pathway column and answers the next column. Split it between the two.", "The references name the same body twice: \"Environmental Protection Agency. (2020).\" and \"U.S. Environmental Protection Agency. (2025).\" Use one form.", "\"Developed by IFFCO, Sagarika is a microbial biostimulant\". Check this against the Fertilizer Control Order source you cite; if Sagarika is seaweed-derived like Kelpak, say so, because your comparison of the two cases depends on it.", "Figure 4 labels the Taiwanese category \"plant auxiliary products\" while the text calls it \"植物生長輔助劑類 (Plant Growth Auxiliary Materials)\". Use one label in both places.", "The four United States questions are listed, then only three get an answer; \"Which state-level fertilizer or beneficial-substance requirements apply?\" never gets its own paragraph. Answer all four in the order you asked them.", "Abstract sheet map, row 4: \"EU reference point\" describes only subsection 4.1. Write \"What each molecule does, and the EU biostimulant category\".", "Abstract sheet map, row 11: \"five questions\" starts in lower case and says little. Write \"Five questions that sum up our strategy\".", "Abstract sheet, Components: \"Bioreactor · Equipment · Separate equipment pathway\" says equipment twice and nothing else. Write \"Own safety and conformity check, set by the final hardware\"."]}, "live": "https://2026.igem.wiki/gems-taiwan/", "mockPages": ["human-practices", "education", "entrepreneurship", "sustainability", "laws-and-regulations", "geospatial-analysis", "data-physicalization"], "wiki": "5d10698, 2026-10-09", "notes": [{"id": "law-a01", "zone": "abstract", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "ReLeaf is a three-part system: the bioreactor, the engineered production culture", "title": "Lead with what the page found", "body": "This lead describes the product, and a judge already knows the product by now. The first line of a law page should say where ReLeaf stands. Use your own findings from sections 9 and 10.", "after": "Our cell-free output is aimed at fertilizer and biostimulant routes, while the engineered culture and the bioreactor need separate approvals, and Taiwan's contained-use route is still unconfirmed.", "excerpts": ["gis-2024-og-marburg-01", "laws-2025-og-wur-01"], "excerpt_note": "Marburg gives problem, method, result and risk in four sentences. WageningenUR puts the rule that changes the design in its first sentence."}, {"id": "law-a02", "zone": "abstract", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "The same biological output may be treated as a fertilizer, plant biostimulant, or pesticide", "title": "The bold stops before the point", "body": "Only \"The same biological output may be treated\" is bold, so a skimming judge reads half a sentence. The message worth remembering is what decides the category. Bold that part.", "after": "The same output may count as a fertilizer, a biostimulant or a pesticide: **what we claim it does decides which**."}, {"id": "law-a03", "zone": "abstract", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "Our analysis focuses on classification, containment, efficacy, and market access", "title": "Show who you asked", "body": "The sheet names laws and markets but no person. Judges look for stakeholders on the first screen. Name the Cheng Han Biotechnology briefing here, and any regulator you ask before the freeze.", "after": "We checked primary legal sources and a regulatory briefing from Yuan Chiu-Ying of Cheng Han Biotechnology (9 July 2026), focusing on classification, containment, efficacy and market access.", "excerpts": ["ihp-2025-og-wur-01"], "excerpt_note": "WageningenUR's opening states the purpose, how many people the team engaged and of which kinds, all in three sentences.", "ballot": "How well did it incorporate different stakeholder views?"}, {"id": "law-a04", "zone": "abstract", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "This makes PFC 6(B) a potential pathway for", "title": "This sentence was cut from its context", "body": "In the body this sentence follows the EU definition of PFC 6. Here \"This\" points at nothing, and PFC 6(B) is never explained. A judge outside the EU will not know the code. Say what it is in the same sentence.", "after": "In the EU, our output could fit PFC 6(B), the category for non-microbial plant biostimulants, but that is **not an automatic classification**."}, {"id": "law-a05", "zone": "abstract", "type": "praise", "anchor": "but not an automatic classification", "title": "The right message in bold", "body": "If a judge remembers one thing from this sheet, it should be that a possible route is not an approval. You chose that as your bold message, and it is the line that makes the page trustworthy.", "excerpts": ["laws-2024-ug-waterloo-01"], "excerpt_note": "Waterloo writes the same caution as an if-then sentence."}, {"id": "law-a06", "zone": "abstract", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "Contained-Use / Biotechnology Framework", "title": "These rows read as decided", "body": "This row, and the cell-free row above it (\"Fertilizer/Plant Biostimulant\"), name a route with no hedge. The body calls both routes \"under investigation\", and the last row says contained use in Taiwan is unconfirmed. Add the status to each line.", "after": "Contained-use rules, to be confirmed in each market"}, {"id": "law-a07", "zone": "abstract", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "Taiwan → European Union → Philippines", "title": "Market order disagrees with Entrepreneurship", "body": "This row and the paragraph (\"the EU as our current first international market\") both put the EU second. The Entrepreneurship page makes the Philippines the first expansion in year 3 and puts the European Union in \"Global market expansion Year 5.5 - Year 6.0+\". This is now on the first screen of both pages, so fix it first.", "after": "Taiwan → [first market] → [second market], the same order as the Entrepreneurship page"}, {"id": "law-a08", "zone": "abstract", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Applicable contained-use framework remains unconfirmed", "title": "An open question on the first screen", "body": "You put an unsolved problem in the summary, marked Open. Most teams hide that kind of line deep in the page. It tells the judge you know where your case is weak.", "excerpts": ["laws-2024-og-uzurich-03"], "excerpt_note": "UZurich states its hardest legal result in one plain sentence."}, {"id": "law-001", "type": "praise", "anchor": "We examined how each component is treated in Taiwan, the European Union, the United States", "title": "The page opens with its scope", "body": "Your first two sentences split ReLeaf into three parts and name the four places you checked. A judge reads one line and knows what the page covers and how it is organised. Most law pages open with general sentences about regulation and never say what they examined."}, {"id": "law-002", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "with Taiwan as the development environment and the EU as our current first international market", "title": "Market order disagrees with Entrepreneurship", "body": "Here the EU is \"our current first international market to investigate\". The Entrepreneurship page puts the European Union in \"Global market expansion Year 5.5 - Year 6.0+\" and makes the Philippines the first expansion in year 3. Pick one order and write the same one on both pages.", "after": "with Taiwan as the development environment and the EU as the first market we studied in depth. Our planned market order is [order], the same order given on the Entrepreneurship page.", "excerpts": ["laws-2025-og-wur-06"], "excerpt_note": "WageningenUR repeats the same regulatory figure and the same source on its business page, so the two pages agree."}, {"id": "law-003", "type": "cut", "anchor": "We examined these components across Taiwan, the European Union, the United States", "title": "This paragraph repeats the abstract", "body": "Section 2 opens by saying again what section 1 just said, almost word for word. Cut it and start section 2 with the chain that follows, which is new.", "after": "Our regulatory analysis follows: Function - Claim - Classification - Requirement - Market Access."}, {"id": "law-004", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "Abiotic-stress positioning makes fertilizer/biostimulant pathways the main strategy under investigation", "title": "Say the principle in plain words", "body": "\"Abiotic-stress positioning\" is your own shorthand, and a judge often reads the table before the prose. Write the principle as cause and effect.", "after": "Because we claim stress tolerance and no pest control, the fertilizer and biostimulant routes are our main strategy."}, {"id": "law-005", "type": "praise", "anchor": "The regulatory information from this briefing was used as an industry perspective", "title": "This caption sources itself properly", "body": "The caption names the researcher, her title, her company, the date of the briefing, and what you did with what she said. It also marks the briefing as an industry view beside your primary sources. A judge can check every part of that.", "excerpts": ["laws-2025-og-wur-03"], "excerpt_note": "WageningenUR names the regulator's staff, the question asked and the answer in the same paragraph.", "ballot": "How well did it incorporate different stakeholder views?"}, {"id": "law-006", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "separately assesses GMO/GMM transport, storage, and waste requirements", "title": "Write out GMM once", "body": "GMO is expanded nowhere on the page and GMM appears only here. An international judge may know GMO and not GMM. Give both in full the first time.", "after": "Keeps the culture inside the production system, and separately assesses transport, storage and waste rules for genetically modified organisms and micro-organisms (GMOs and GMMs)."}, {"id": "law-007", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Containment is not only an engineering requirement; it determines the exposure pathway", "title": "One sentence carries the whole argument", "body": "This sentence ties your hardware to the law: containment decides what a regulator has to consider. Everything later on the page depends on it, and you state it once, clearly. Keep it where it is."}, {"id": "law-008", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "also allows the two components be assessed as distinct regulatory questions", "title": "Missing word in the last sentence", "body": "It should read \"to be assessed\". The sentence is also long. Split it.", "after": "Separating the engineered production organism from the cell-free output lets regulators assess the two as distinct questions."}, {"id": "law-009", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "The hollow-fiber membrane forms part of this separation", "title": "Name the detail that decides containment", "body": "You say the membrane holds the culture back, but not what a regulator would measure. Give the pore size, say whether live cells or DNA can leave with the output, and name the limit you would have to meet. Link to Safety and Hardware, where the evidence sits.", "after": "The hollow-fiber membrane ([pore size]) retains the production culture and lets the soluble products through. No live cells leave the loop, and residual DNA in the output is [number], measured as described on our Safety page.", "excerpts": ["laws-2024-og-marburg-04", "ihp-2025-og-wur-05"], "excerpt_note": "Marburg found the one design detail (a 34 bp scar) that decides its legal category. WageningenUR asked its regulator for the residual DNA threshold and got 0.9%, dead or alive cells.", "ballot": "To what extent is the Human Practices work documented so that others can build upon it?"}, {"id": "law-010", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "The three molecules are treated as biostimulants", "title": "Two hedges in one paragraph", "body": "This paragraph hedges the word biostimulant twice and in three long sentences. Say it once, in two short ones. The table below also heads its first column \"Biostimulant\", which undoes the hedge; call that column \"Molecule\".", "after": "We call these three molecules biostimulants because of what they do in the plant. No authority has classified them, so the column below gives the category we are aiming at."}, {"id": "law-011", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "Our intended function is therefore abiotic-stress resilience rather than control of pathogens, insects, or weeds", "title": "NaD1 is missing from this page", "body": "The Entrepreneurship page lists NaD1 (antifungal defence) as one of the three proteins the bioreactor makes, and plans a \"biopesticide designation for NaD1\". Your page names three molecules and rules out pathogen claims. Say what happened to NaD1: dropped, or kept with a separate pesticide route.", "after": "Our intended function is abiotic-stress resilience. We make no claim about pathogens, insects or weeds. [NaD1 is no longer part of the product / NaD1 would need a separate pesticide route, described in section [n]]."}, {"id": "law-012", "type": "praise", "anchor": "This makes PFC 6(B) a potential pathway for ReLeaf", "title": "A pathway named without claiming it", "body": "You name a route and then refuse to claim it. The difference between a possible route and an approval is exactly what a judge checks on a law page, and you mark it.", "excerpts": ["laws-2024-ug-waterloo-01"], "excerpt_note": "Waterloo writes the same uncertainty as an if-then sentence, which is the shape you could use for your Taiwan paragraphs too."}, {"id": "law-013", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "PFC 6(B) covers non-microbial plant biostimulants", "title": "Explain why a cell-free output is non-microbial", "body": "PFC 6(B) is for non-microbial biostimulants, and your product is made by a micro-organism. A reader will stop here. Give the reason in one sentence, and say what cell or DNA limit you would have to meet.", "after": "PFC 6(B) covers non-microbial plant biostimulants. Our output contains no cells, so we are investigating it under PFC 6(B), which would require us to show no live cells and no more than [limit] residual DNA.", "excerpts": ["ihp-2025-og-wur-05"], "excerpt_note": "This is the question WageningenUR put to RIVM, and the answer it got back decided their downstream processing."}, {"id": "law-014", "type": "cut", "anchor": "current biostimulants are being investigated for complementary abiotic-stress functions", "title": "The molecule list appears six times", "body": "BoPep4 for salinity, AtLEA14 for drought and ACCD for heat are listed in sections 4, 4.1, Taiwan, EU, 9, 10 and 11. Give the list once in the table in section 4 and refer back to it after that.", "after": "The three molecules are being investigated for the abiotic-stress functions in Table [n]. The final product must also satisfy applicable composition, safety, efficacy, labeling, and conformity requirements."}, {"id": "law-015", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "Taiwan is our development and validation environment.", "title": "Say what you actually do in Taiwan", "body": "\"Development and validation environment\" tells a judge very little. Name the work: the lab, the plant trials, the farmer meetings, and the first registration.", "after": "Taiwan is where we build and test ReLeaf. Our lab work, plant trials and farmer meetings are all here, and it is where we would register first."}, {"id": "law-016", "type": "praise", "anchor": "fertilizer specifications recognize 植物生長輔助劑類 (Plant Growth Auxiliary Materials) among its fertilizer categories", "title": "You give the Taiwanese term both ways", "body": "The Chinese category name with an English translation lets a judge outside Taiwan search the real term, and a Taiwanese reader see the exact wording. Do this for every Taiwanese law and category you name."}, {"id": "law-017", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "The final formulation and claims would need to be characterized and confirmed with the Ministry of Agriculture.", "title": "Name the agencies and their laws", "body": "Judges from other countries do not know how Taiwan's Ministry of Agriculture is split. Your Figure 2 has the answer, so put it in the text once, before you rely on it.", "after": "Taiwan's Ministry of Agriculture oversees both routes. The Agriculture and Food Agency registers fertilizers under the Fertilizer Management Act, and the Animal and Plant Health Inspection Agency handles pesticides under the Pesticide Management Act. We would confirm our formulation and claims with the Agriculture and Food Agency.", "excerpts": ["laws-2024-og-marburg-12"], "excerpt_note": "Marburg says who sets a rule and who decides the details, which is what a reader needs before the rule itself."}, {"id": "law-018", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "If registered as a fertilizer, ReLeaf would need to establish a defined and reproducible product composition", "title": "Timeline disagrees with Entrepreneurship", "body": "Your page says the category still needs confirmation from the Ministry of Agriculture. The Entrepreneurship page says the fertilizer route takes \"approximately 20 days for fertilizer specification report plus 7 days for government review\" and brings \"early revenue within 30 days\". Both cannot be true. Carry the same durations and the same source onto both pages.", "after": "If registered as a fertilizer, ReLeaf would need a defined and reproducible composition, safety and quality data, efficacy data, labeling and registration information. [Name] told us the review itself takes about [number] days once the file is complete.", "excerpts": ["laws-2025-og-wur-04", "laws-2025-og-wur-06"], "excerpt_note": "WageningenUR's 2.5 to 3.5 year approval figure appears on the IHP page and the business timeline with the same attribution.", "ballot": "How well was the Human Practices work integrated throughout the project?"}, {"id": "law-019", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Our current review did not identify a general Taiwan contained-use GMO framework", "title": "You say what you could not find", "body": "You report a gap in your own research instead of guessing. This is the most honest line on the page, and it costs nothing. Keep the sentence and add the date of the review.", "excerpts": ["laws-2024-og-uzurich-03"], "excerpt_note": "UZurich states plainly that its product could not be used in its own country. Judges rewarded that page."}, {"id": "law-020", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "We therefore treat its regulatory status as unconfirmed.", "title": "Entrepreneurship promises what this page cannot find", "body": "This page finds no Taiwan contained-use framework that resolves your case. The Entrepreneurship page says \"Taiwan's regulatory framework contains a critical pathway: if engineered bacteria never leave the sealed device, the system can be classified as contained-use equipment\", and expects \"absolute containment\" certification by the end of year 2. One page says the route is not there, the other sells a certificate from it. Fix both.", "after": "We therefore treat its regulatory status as unconfirmed. Before commercial deployment we would ask [agency] whether a sealed system counts as contained use, which is the question our business plan depends on."}, {"id": "law-021", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "The bioreactor is an equipment rather than the agricultural product itself.", "title": "Equipment is uncountable here", "body": "Write \"is equipment\", with no \"an\". You could also make the sentence do more work.", "after": "The bioreactor is equipment, and not the agricultural product itself."}, {"id": "law-022", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "heat-stress mitigation evidence for ACCD", "title": "ACCD is tested on salt elsewhere", "body": "Here ACCD carries the heat claim. The Geospatial Analysis page reports ACCD evidence under salt stress, \"holding onto 86.3% of unstressed performance at moderate salinity\". If your ACCD data is salinity data, the claim you can support today is salinity. Match the claim to the experiment on every page, and link to Results.", "after": "The intended claim decides the evidence we need. Our ACCD data so far is [salinity / heat] data, reported on our Results page, and the [other] claim still has no supporting experiment."}, {"id": "law-023", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "For ReLeaf, the main question remain:", "title": "Plural verb and a missing word", "body": "Three questions follow, so it should read \"the main questions remain\". There are four bullets under it, not three.", "after": "For ReLeaf, four questions remain:"}, {"id": "law-024", "type": "praise", "anchor": "40 C.F.R. Part 725) is a principal federal framework to investigate", "title": "You cite the law where you use it", "body": "This is the one place on the page where the instrument and the part number sit next to the claim. No winner page in our casebook does that. Do the same for your EU and Taiwan claims, with the article number and a link.", "excerpts": ["laws-2024-ug-waterloo-02"], "excerpt_note": "Waterloo puts a numbered source on each sentence of its regulator paragraph."}, {"id": "law-025", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "No single harmonized federal biostimulant pathway", "title": "This table needs a title and a home", "body": "The EU and US comparison arrives with no caption, no header on its first column, and no Taiwan column, and it sits under the \"Equipment\" heading as if it belonged to the United States. Give it a number and a title, label the first column, add Taiwan, and move it to the end of section 5.", "after": "Table [n]. The three target markets compared. First column: Topic. Then Taiwan, European Union, United States."}, {"id": "law-026", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "After Taiwan and the EU, ReLeaf would investigate selected Southeast Asian markets", "title": "Expansion table mixes two lists", "body": "The table under this sentence is headed by priority, yet priority 1 is Taiwan, your development environment, and the EU is missing although your sequence puts it second. Start the table at the Philippines and keep Taiwan and the EU in section 5.", "after": "After Taiwan and the EU, we would enter selected Southeast Asian markets one at a time. The table ranks those markets; Taiwan and the EU are covered in section 5."}, {"id": "law-027", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "The horizontal axis represents the clarity with which ReLeaf could identify the competent authority", "title": "Caption does not match the chart", "body": "The chart's own axes are labelled regulatory and commercialization challenge, and strategic market value for ReLeaf. Your caption describes different axes, and it names five markets while the table lists eleven. Rewrite the caption to match the figure. The last sentence, which says the positions are your own synthesis, is the right kind of honesty and should stay.", "after": "Figure 7. Five expansion markets placed by regulatory and commercialization challenge (horizontal) against strategic value for ReLeaf (vertical). The positions are our qualitative synthesis of the frameworks we reviewed, not official ratings or approval predictions."}, {"id": "law-028", "type": "cut", "anchor": "Fertilizer regulation exists, but current market and regulatory conditions require further investigation", "title": "Six rows say the same thing", "body": "Rows 6 to 11 all say a fertilizer framework exists and the exact route needs investigation. Keep rows 1 to 5, which carry your priorities, and replace the rest with one sentence.", "after": "Malaysia, Cambodia, Laos, Myanmar, Singapore and Brunei all have fertilizer registration systems, and each would need its own check of product category and GMO requirements. We placed them below our priority markets because of [reason]."}, {"id": "law-029", "type": "cut", "anchor": "This sequence is based on regulatory fit and development readiness rather than market size alone.", "title": "Eighteen sentences turn on one phrase", "body": "\"rather than\" appears 18 times on this page. Readers start to hear the same sentence shape every paragraph. Change most of them to a plain statement.", "after": "We ranked this sequence by regulatory fit and development readiness, not by market size."}, {"id": "law-030", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Kelpak provides a useful case study for understanding why ReLeaf cannot assume", "title": "A real product anchors the argument", "body": "You take one product already on the market and show it treated three ways in three places. That proves your point about classification better than any general statement, and it gives a judge something checkable.", "excerpts": ["laws-2024-og-marburg-12"], "excerpt_note": "Marburg compares countries on one concrete measure, which is the same move: one thing, several jurisdictions."}, {"id": "law-031", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "In the European Union, Kelpak is positioned within the EU fertilising-products framework", "title": "These paragraphs retell the table", "body": "The Kelpak table already gives each market, its treatment and what it shows. The two paragraphs after it say the same three things in longer form. Keep the table, cut the retelling, and end with your conclusion.", "after": "The table shows the pattern: Kelpak does not change when it crosses a border, but its pathway does. We therefore treat each target market as a separate regulatory assessment."}, {"id": "law-032", "type": "cut", "anchor": "For ReLeaf, this reinforces the importance of establishing product identity", "title": "Duplicate sentence after Sagarika", "body": "The paragraph above already says this, with \"defining\" in place of \"establishing\". Cut one of the two.", "after": "(Delete this line; the same point is made inside the paragraph above.)"}, {"id": "law-033", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "Our exchange with Cheng Han Biotechnology supplemented our legal research with industry experience", "title": "Write the briefing as a full entry", "body": "This is your only stakeholder on the page, and the section gives no name, no question and no change. Figure 3 already has the name and the date. Write the five parts: who you asked, what you asked, what she said, what it means for which component, and what you changed. Add the meeting to the Human Practices page too, which records your other expert meetings.", "after": "On 9 July 2026 we asked Yuan Chiu-Ying, researcher at Cheng Han Biotechnology, how a cell-free protein product is classified in Taiwan and the EU. She said [answer]. For our cell-free output this meant [consequence], so we [change]. We used her briefing as an industry view beside the primary sources.", "excerpts": ["laws-2025-og-wur-03", "laws-2025-og-wur-04"], "excerpt_note": "Both WageningenUR passages run the full entry in under 150 words, ending with what the team changed.", "ballot": "How well did it incorporate different stakeholder views?"}, {"id": "law-034", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "BoPEP4, AtLEA14, and ACCD are being developed to address complementary abiotic stresses", "title": "Two spellings of the same peptide", "body": "This page writes BoPEP4 seven times and BoPep4 three times. Every other ReLeaf page writes BoPep4. Change all of them to BoPep4 and search the page for the other spelling.", "after": "BoPep4, AtLEA14, and ACCD are being developed to address complementary abiotic stresses."}, {"id": "law-035", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "Organic production standards may restrict genetically modified organisms and certain inputs.", "title": "Organic rules are stricter than this", "body": "The Geospatial Analysis page states it as a hard exclusion: \"Taiwan's Organic Agriculture Promotion Act excludes any product of a genetically modified organism from certified organic production, regardless of containment\", and puts the lost land at about 3.7%. Your row says standards \"may restrict\". Name the Act here and state the exclusion, since it is a market limit a judge will look for.", "after": "Taiwan's Organic Agriculture Promotion Act excludes any product of a genetically modified organism from certified organic production, whatever the containment. Certified organic farming is therefore closed to ReLeaf, about 3.7% of cultivated land."}, {"id": "law-036", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "Final TSCA Part 725 pathway remains to be determined", "title": "Give each open item an owner", "body": "This table is the strongest section on the page, and every row stops at the problem. Add two columns: who you would ask, and the next step with a date. An open question with a plan reads as work in progress.", "after": "Issue: US engineered culture. Status: final TSCA Part 725 pathway undetermined. Who we would ask: [body]. Next step: [action] by [date].", "excerpts": ["laws-2024-og-uzurich-02"], "excerpt_note": "UZurich gives a pending rule its deadline and its current status in two sentences.", "ballot": "To what extent is the Human Practices work documented so that others can build upon it?"}, {"id": "law-037", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "regulatory strategy can therefore be reduced to five questions", "title": "Move these five questions up", "body": "These are the best lines on the page, and they arrive last. Put them at the top, as the questions the page answers, and let section 11 give the answers. A reader can then hold every section against a question.", "after": "(Open the page with the five questions, then answer them in order: What does ReLeaf do? What will we claim? Which category could contain those claims? What requirements follow? Is the market practical?)", "excerpts": ["laws-2024-og-marburg-01", "laws-2024-og-uzurich-01"], "excerpt_note": "Both winners open with the questions their page then answers in order."}, {"id": "law-038", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "Promulgated June 16, 1999; last amended June 19, 2002", "title": "Add article numbers and real links", "body": "Your references give bare domains, and no claim in the text carries an article number. Put the article beside each claim, give the full link and the date you read it, and add an \"as of\" date for rules still changing, such as Indonesia's PP No. 7/2026. Your casebook found no winning page that does this, so it is the cheapest way for your page to stand out.", "after": "Ministry of Agriculture, Taiwan. Fertilizer Management Act, Article [n] (promulgated 16 June 1999, last amended 19 June 2002). [full URL], accessed [date].", "excerpts": ["laws-2024-ug-waterloo-02"], "excerpt_note": "Waterloo's numbered, dated government references are the minimum; the article number next to the claim is the part you can beat them on.", "ballot": "To what extent is the Human Practices work documented so that others can build upon it?"}], "excerpts": {"gis-2024-og-marburg-01": {"id": "gis-2024-og-marburg-01", "prize": "gis", "team": "Marburg", "year": 2024, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/marburg/gis", "section": "Abstract", "function": "page-opening", "text": "The global demand for natural rubber is reshaping the landscape of agricultural cultivation, driving expansion into new regions and increasing environmental risks. Using high-resolution geospatial analysis, we mapped the global distribution of Hevea brasiliensis (natural rubber) cultivation under current and future climate scenarios, generating the most detailed model to date. This analysis highlights shifts in suitable cultivation zones driven by climate change and identifies key ecological challenges, including the potential spread of the devastating South American Leaf Blight (SALB).", "why_it_works": "Problem, method, result and the risk found, in four sentences. A reader who stops here knows what was mapped, at what scale and why it matters.", "teaches": ["structure", "concision"], "prizeName": "GIS page"}, "ihp-2025-og-wur-01": {"id": "ihp-2025-og-wur-01", "prize": "ihp", "team": "WageningenUR", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/wageningenur/human-practices/index.html", "section": "INTEGRATED HUMAN PRACTICES", "function": "page-opening", "text": "BCoated is developed to provide a real-world solution that can help agriculture move towards increased productivity and sustainability. Human Practices (HP) guided all developmental stages of our product, helping us pinpoint issues in agriculture and the contribution we can make to alleviate them. Through over 35 diverse engagements, including in-depth interviews, facility visits, and participation in events, we built a holistic understanding of the complex intersection of challenges faced by arable farmers, sustainable agriculture, biomanufacturing, and regulatory frameworks.", "why_it_works": "States the product's purpose, a count of engagements and the kinds of engagement in three sentences, then names the four fields the work had to fit (farmers, agriculture, biomanufacturing, regulation).", "teaches": ["audience-framing", "concision", "evidence"], "prizeName": "IHP"}, "ihp-2025-og-wur-05": {"id": "ihp-2025-og-wur-05", "prize": "ihp", "team": "WageningenUR", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/wageningenur/human-practices/index.html", "section": "#7.2 Insights from regulatory institutions", "function": "evidence-with-numbers", "text": "We spoke to RIVM ( Samia Ouhajji, Casper Jongerkrijg, Dr. Jeroen Roelfsema ) to understand whether our various tuned BC coatings with different properties would require separate approvals. Samia Ouhajji explained that chemicals can be grouped for approval, and BC can be registered as a chemical. This simplifies the approval procedure for our product. However, each toxic protein module still requires its own independent risk assessment, ensuring that there will be no off-target effects. That means some of our functionalisations would require a separate assessment. They also emphasised the 0.9% GMO DNA threshold in the final products, regardless of whether the cells are dead or alive. This will impact the downstream processing of our product.", "why_it_works": "Says why the team asked, what the regulator answered, and which part of the product each answer touches. The 0.9% threshold is tied directly to a consequence for downstream processing.", "teaches": ["evidence", "loop-closing", "assumed-knowledge"], "prizeName": "IHP"}, "laws-2024-og-marburg-01": {"id": "laws-2024-og-marburg-01", "prize": "laws", "team": "Marburg", "year": 2024, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/marburg/human-practices/legal-political", "section": "Political Dimension", "function": "page-opening", "text": "Considering the impact of political decisions is crucial for the practical application of any project in biotechnology. Key questions arise, such as: What regulations must be adhered to? What restrictions exist on the organisms used in production? And, what is the current stance on genetically modified organisms (GMOs)?", "why_it_works": "Opens with three questions the reader can test the page against: which rules apply, what limits the organism, what the public stance is. The page then answers them in order.", "teaches": ["structure", "audience-framing", "clarity"], "prizeName": "Law page"}, "laws-2024-og-marburg-04": {"id": "laws-2024-og-marburg-04", "prize": "laws", "team": "Marburg", "year": 2024, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/marburg/human-practices/legal-political/laibach-hoffie", "section": "Inclusion in Our Project", "function": "loop-closing", "text": "Thanks to the interview we learned a lot about the upcoming EU law regarding plants generated through NGTs. Furthermore, we were able to contextualize our project within the NGT framework. We now know that our CRISPR approach falls under category 1. However, if the Cre-lox sequence is too long, our plants would be classified under category 2. Potential future relaxations for category 2 plants that support sustainability goals might ease regulatory pressure on these plants too.", "why_it_works": "Places the project in a legal category and names the one design detail (the Cre-lox scar length) that could move it to the stricter category. This is the \"what it means for us\" slot done at the level of a single construct.", "teaches": ["loop-closing", "specificity", "evidence"], "prizeName": "Law page"}, "laws-2024-og-marburg-12": {"id": "laws-2024-og-marburg-12", "prize": "laws", "team": "Marburg", "year": 2024, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/marburg/safety-security", "section": "Navigating Future Safety Considerations in Agriculture", "function": "evidence-with-numbers", "text": "Because there is disagreement on what the ideal strategy for biocontainment is, countries vary in the requirements for buffer zone size. In the US, there is no federal law requiring buffer zones around GM fields, although some regulations have been passed at State levels. Mostly, the USDA (United States Department of Agriculture) issues guidelines that encourage the implementation of buffer zones, while the exact implementation (type and width) remains for the farmer to decide.", "why_it_works": "Compares jurisdictions on one concrete measure (buffer zones) and says who sets the rule and who decides the details. One measure across countries is easier to follow than a survey of whole legal systems.", "teaches": ["structure", "specificity"], "prizeName": "Law page"}, "laws-2024-og-uzurich-01": {"id": "laws-2024-og-uzurich-01", "prize": "laws", "team": "UZurich", "year": 2024, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/uzurich/integrated-human-practices", "section": "Integrated Human Practices (introduction)", "function": "section-intro", "text": "As genetic engineering in agriculture is a topic of constant debate in Europe and Switzerland, we knew that a third set of questions had to be answered. How is gene technology regulated in Switzerland? What criticism is there regarding the use of GMOs in agriculture? Are there ecological risks to our project? How do consumers perceive our project?", "why_it_works": "Frames the legal chapter as four questions, legal, critical, ecological and social, before naming anyone. The reader knows what each interview is for.", "teaches": ["structure", "audience-framing"], "prizeName": "Law page"}, "laws-2024-og-uzurich-02": {"id": "laws-2024-og-uzurich-02", "prize": "laws", "team": "UZurich", "year": 2024, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/uzurich/integrated-human-practices", "section": "3. Ethics, Politics, Law and Consumer Perception", "function": "evidence-with-numbers", "text": "That the Federal Council was actually requested to draft a decree for a risk-based authorization regulation for the agricultural plant cultivation of GMOs without transgenic genetic material by mid-2024. This has not yet happened.", "why_it_works": "Reports a pending legal change with its deadline and its current status in two sentences. The reader can tell the rule is in motion and how far it has moved.", "teaches": ["evidence", "concision"], "prizeName": "Law page"}, "laws-2024-og-uzurich-03": {"id": "laws-2024-og-uzurich-03", "prize": "laws", "team": "UZurich", "year": 2024, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/uzurich/integrated-human-practices", "section": "3. Ethics, Politics, Law and Consumer Perception", "function": "limitation", "text": "The discussion with Dr. Jürg Niklaus was very informative as we learned that for many people there is a difference between transgenic and cisgenic methods and that these methods should be regulated differently. We also learned that currently our project could not be legally implemented in Switzerland due to the moratorium.", "why_it_works": "States the hard legal result plainly: the product could not be used in the team's own country. Bad news told in one sentence reads as honesty, and judges reward it.", "teaches": ["limitation", "voice", "clarity"], "prizeName": "Law page"}, "laws-2024-ug-waterloo-01": {"id": "laws-2024-ug-waterloo-01", "prize": "laws", "team": "Waterloo", "year": 2024, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/waterloo/policy-research", "section": "Antibiotic Policy", "function": "other", "text": "In the case of BovEco, it is unclear whether the Canadian government agencies would class a lytic enzyme and an antibiotic due to its biodegradability as a protein. Should it be classed as an antibiotic, it would, like ionophores, fall into the class 4 category due to its specific targeting of archaea which are not associated with pathogenicity to humans.", "why_it_works": "Writes an unresolved classification as a conditional: it is unclear how the regulator would class the enzyme; if classed as an antibiotic, it would land in a named class for a stated reason.", "teaches": ["clarity", "evidence"], "prizeName": "Law page"}, "laws-2024-ug-waterloo-02": {"id": "laws-2024-ug-waterloo-02", "prize": "laws", "team": "Waterloo", "year": 2024, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/waterloo/policy-research", "section": "Canadian Food Inspection Agency (CFIA) Regulations", "function": "definition", "text": "The CFIA also mandates a rigorous safety assessment process for genetically modified organisms (GMOs) used in animal feed [3]. This assessment, required before any GMO feed can be approved, evaluates both the nutritional adequacy for livestock and the potential environmental impacts, including effects on biodiversity, ensuring compliance with national feed safety standards [3].", "why_it_works": "Says what the regulator checks before a GM feed can be approved (nutrition and environmental effects) and when (before approval), with a numbered source on each sentence. Short and checkable.", "teaches": ["evidence", "concision"], "prizeName": "Law page"}, "laws-2025-og-wur-01": {"id": "laws-2025-og-wur-01", "prize": "laws", "team": "WageningenUR", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/wageningenur/human-practices/index.html", "section": "#3 Choice of the platform material for the coating and EU regulations", "function": "section-intro", "text": "Traditionally, seed coatings have relied on petroleum-based binders (adhesives, or “seed stickers”). This is a practice soon to be outlawed, as we found out from our stakeholders in the seed sector. Our consultation with Amalia Kafka from Euroseeds highlighted the urgency of this change, due to the upcoming EU microplastics restrictions under the EU chemical legislation REACH:", "why_it_works": "Law enters as a pressure on the design in the first sentence, through a named source. The reader learns the current practice, that it will be banned, and who told the team.", "teaches": ["structure", "evidence", "loop-closing"], "prizeName": "Law page"}, "laws-2025-og-wur-03": {"id": "laws-2025-og-wur-03", "prize": "laws", "team": "WageningenUR", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/wageningenur/human-practices/index.html", "section": "#7.2 Insights from regulatory institutions", "function": "stakeholder-entry", "text": "We spoke to RIVM ( Samia Ouhajji, Casper Jongerkrijg, Dr. Jeroen Roelfsema ) to understand whether our various tuned BC coatings with different properties would require separate approvals. Samia Ouhajji explained that chemicals can be grouped for approval, and BC can be registered as a chemical. This simplifies the approval procedure for our product. However, each toxic protein module still requires its own independent risk assessment, ensuring that there will be no off-target effects. That means some of our functionalisations would require a separate assessment. They also emphasised the 0.9% GMO DNA threshold in the final products, regardless of whether the cells are dead or alive. This will impact the downstream processing of our product. Bio Base Europe gave some suggestions on how we could achieve this without high costs, but more research will have to go into this.", "why_it_works": "Names the regulator's staff, the question asked, the answer, the threshold (0.9% GMO DNA, dead or alive cells) and the consequence for downstream processing. It ends by admitting the problem is not solved.", "teaches": ["evidence", "specificity", "loop-closing"], "prizeName": "Law page"}, "laws-2025-og-wur-04": {"id": "laws-2025-og-wur-04", "prize": "laws", "team": "WageningenUR", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/wageningenur/human-practices/index.html", "section": "#7.2 Insights from regulatory institutions", "function": "loop-closing", "text": "At the European level, we spoke with the European Commission - DG SANTE - E4 Unit. They clarified that under Regulation (EC) No 283/2013, our bioinsecticide protein would need EU-level approval as an active substance (2.5–3.5 years), followed by authorisation at the Member State level. While amendments are under discussion to ease access for protein-based biocontrols, the process remains resource-intensive. This shaped our business plan: we included regulatory timelines and emphasised partnerships with established seed companies to share the financial burden of trials.", "why_it_works": "All five slots in under 90 words: the regulation by number, the approval route, the duration, the confirming body, and the change to the business plan.", "teaches": ["loop-closing", "concision", "evidence"], "prizeName": "Law page"}, "laws-2025-og-wur-06": {"id": "laws-2025-og-wur-06", "prize": "laws", "team": "WageningenUR", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/wageningenur/entrepreneurship/index.html", "section": "Timeline", "function": "transition", "text": "We have also presented a timeline ranging from the present to 2042. In the timeline, we give a detailed description of our journey from an iGEM team to a fully operational company, highlighting important milestones. We also took input from our stakeholders into account. For example, representatives of the RIVM and European Commision told us that the legal approval of a novel seed coating can take up to three years.", "why_it_works": "The regulator's approval time reappears in the business timeline with the same attribution. A judge reading only the Entrepreneurship page still meets the legal constraint.", "teaches": ["consistency", "loop-closing"], "prizeName": "Law page"}}};
